  "query_preview": "..\n\nSynoptic Setup: Forecast begins with relatively zonal to NW flow\nfrom Montana through the PCNW and across SW Canada. To the S, hot\nhigh pressure sits over the desert southwest with a ridge \nbuilding into the Intermountain West. Farther West a highly \namplified trough sit south of the Aleutian Islands. \n\nToday through Thursday night: Zonal to NW flow remains relatively\nintact with temps remaining 5 to 15 degrees above normal with \nrelatively dry conditions. There is a small chance during this \ntime that any fires out west could begin to pool smoke across the \narea, but HRRR and RAP Smoke models suggest nothing more than \nlight haze. Meanwhile, the Intermountain West ridge will drift \neast and the crest will start to bump up against MT while the \nAleutian trough behind it will begin slamming up against the PCNW\ncoast. \n\nFriday: Ridge crest will move across eastern Montana raising \nhighs to 10 to 20 degrees above normal with highs into the 70s and\n80s. There is a chance that some chinook winds could begin to dry\nout the atmosphere a bit more than normal as well here leading to\npossible fire weather concerns. This ridge will also be monitored\nfor breakdown timing. Meanwhile, the PCNW trough will smash into \nthe large ridge and begin digging S into the lower Intermountain \nwest and southern California instead. \n\nFriday night into Monday: Newly minted SW flow regime will drag a\ncold front across Montana Friday night and lead to pacific \nmoisture streaming into the region. By Saturday to Saturday night \nrains showers will begin to form as highs drop into the 40s and \n50s by Sunday or nearly 20 degrees below normal. Expect a strong \nwind behind this frontal passage Saturday. Temps dropping low are\nsuggesting that snow may be possible overnight Sunday night but \ntotals will be less than an inch if it snows at all. Also, warm \nground should melt off all of it. No accumulation would be\nexpected. Cooler, wetter periods holds through about Monday \nbefore beginning to clear out. However, forecasts beyond Saturday \ndo become in question as ensemble data moves out of phase with \neach other. In addition, the ridge to the east appears to become \npersistent and blocks any progress in the flow which may mean the \ncooler and wetter air could hold on if the trough is delayed its \nexit to the east. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n- No changes from NBM.\n\n- There is moderate to low confidence (30-50%) in frontal timing\n  Friday night. This could easily shift up to 12 hours in future\n  forecasts.\n\n- There is high confidence (70-90%) in warm temperatures through \n  Friday and cooler temps for Sunday/Monday.\n\n- There is moderate to high confidence (50-80%) in any precip\n  occuring Saturday night through Monday morning.",
